<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Danube Sich</h1><p class="st-meta">1775 CE – 1828 CE · capital Dunavets</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The refuge of Zaporozhian Cossacks in the Ottoman Danube Delta after Catherine the Great destroyed their Sich.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the Russian army abolished the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zaporozhian-sich">Zaporozhian Sich</a> in 1775, several thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> fled to Ottoman territory. After moving between several sites, they settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> Delta, where the sultan allowed them to found a new Sich in return for military service. Its main centre from 1814 was at Dunavets, in modern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-romania">Romania</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> Sich kept the old Zaporozhian institutions of an elected kish otaman, a council and kurins. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> fished, farmed and fought in Ottoman campaigns, including against Serbian and Greek rebels. They clashed repeatedly with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nekrasov-cossacks">Nekrasov Cossacks</a>, Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-believers">Old Believers</a> settled nearby, over fishing grounds and Ottoman favour.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1806-1812">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1828, the kish otaman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osyp-hladky">Osyp Hladky</a> defected to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> with part of the Host. In response the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> destroyed the Sich. The defectors formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azov">Azov</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> Host in Russia, while the others dispersed within Ottoman lands.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/danube-sich.jpg" alt="Danube Sich" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Macreanu Iulian · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jurisdic%C5%A3ia_Mitropoliei_Proilaviei_-_p%C3%A2n%C4%83_la_1672.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1775 CE – 1828 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dunavets</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Osyp Hladky</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ukrainian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zadunaiska Sich, Transdanubian Sich</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1802&amp;focus=danube-sich" aria-label="Danube Sich on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5321 617 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Danube Sich lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5641 873L5818 759M5641 873L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5818 759h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5641 873h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5641 873h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Dunavets. <a href="/atlas?year=1802&amp;focus=danube-sich">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
